<header><p class="st-kicker">other · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Dominion of India</h1><p class="st-meta">1947 CE – 1950 CE · capital New Delhi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Independent India's first constitutional form, a Commonwealth dominion that integrated the princely states and drafted the constitution of the republic.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence-act-1947">Indian Independence Act</a> of 1947, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj</a> was partitioned, and on 15 August 1947 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> was created with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> as prime minister. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-mountbatten">Lord Mountbatten</a> stayed on as Governor-General until 1948, succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/c-rajagopalachari">C. Rajagopalachari</a>, the only Indian to hold the post.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dominion confronted partition violence and refugee resettlement, and fought the first war with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dominion-of-pakistan">Dominion of Pakistan</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vallabhbhai-patel">Vallabhbhai Patel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/v-p-menon">V. P. Menon</a> integrated more than five hundred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, using force against Hyderabad in 1948. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> was assassinated in January 1948. A Constituent Assembly chaired in drafting by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> wrote the new constitution.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-india">Constitution of India</a> came into force on 26 January 1950, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> a sovereign republic within the Commonwealth and ending the British monarch's role as head of state. The dominion was succeeded by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dominion-of-india.jpg" alt="Dominion of India" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Government of India · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_India.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1947 CE – 1950 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>New Delhi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jawaharlal Nehru, Lord Mountbatten, C. Rajagopalachari</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hindi, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Union of India (1947-1950), India (dominion)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1948&amp;focus=dominion-of-india" aria-label="Dominion of India on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6698 1130 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dominion of India lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7018 1386L7364 1602M7018 1386L7018 1386M7018 1386L7003 1288M7018 1386L7126 2117M7018 1386L7064 1471M7018 1386L7160 1530M7018 1386L6896 1610M7018 1386L7012 1423M7018 1386L6996 1446M7018 1386L7132 1789M7018 1386L7106 1975M7018 1386L6905 1198M7018 1386L6960 1610M7018 1386L7066 1576M7018 1386L7020 1813M7018 1386L6860 1576M7018 1386L7172 2046M7018 1386L6945 1555M7018 1386L7064 1380M7018 1386L7324 1424M7018 1386L6934 1518M7018 1386L7198 1504M7018 1386L7350 1471M7018 1386L6892 1638M7018 1386L6927 1543M7018 1386L6971 1626M7018 1386L7431 1555M7018 1386L6984 1293M7018 1386L6945 1610M7018 1386L6954 1250M7018 1386L6979 1755M7018 1386L6983 1279M7018 1386L7494 1520M7018 1386L7003 1319M7018 1386L6983 1285M7018 1386L7036 1325M7018